{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-120196",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/1a3ba94aa37bd8b8494794a7013956b100f418c9e0aa7efecab5ba6fc6476ff6.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes become vertical as $y\\to \\pm 1.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(y+1)(y-1)} \\\\ {y}^{\u2032} & =\\frac{1}{1-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{(1+y)(1-y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes become horizontal as $y\\to 0.$\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}$ become horizontal as $y\\to 0.$\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{1-{y}^{2}}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{1}{1-{y}^{2}}$ do *not* become horizontal as $y\\to 0.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}.$",
